Content derivation:
- DISA Security Technical Implementation Guide (STIG) content — rule titles,
  STIG IDs, rule IDs, severities, check text, and fix text — is parsed from the
  official DISA Manual XCCDF releases pinned in stig-src/, whose exact versions,
  benchmark dates, download URLs, and SHA-256 hashes are recorded in
  stig-src/SOURCES.md and stig-src/SHA256SUMS. DISA STIG content is published by
  the U.S. Government and is in the public domain; it is embedded verbatim and
  attributed to its release. Pinned releases as of 2026-09-17:
    RHEL 7  — V3R15, benchmark 24 Jul 2024 (SUNSET at DISA; terminal version)
    RHEL 8  — V2R8,  benchmark 01 Jul 2026
    RHEL 9  — V2R9,  benchmark 01 Jul 2026
    RHEL 10 — V1R2,  benchmark 01 Jul 2026
- DISA Control Correlation Identifier (CCI) List, publish date 2025-01-23, also
  pinned in stig-src/ with its SHA-256. CCI definitions and their NIST SP 800-53
  Revision 5 index values are public-domain U.S. Government content, embedded
  verbatim and filtered to the CCIs the embedded rules actually reference.
- NIST Special Publication 800-53 control identifiers appear as identifiers only,
  by way of the DISA CCI List's own mapping. NIST publications are U.S.
  Government works in the public domain.
- Ansible module schemas, parameter documentation, and CLI flag descriptions are
  extracted programmatically from the locally installed ansible-core
  distribution's own documentation tooling (ansible-doc --json, <cli> --help),
  under its documented usage. Extraction is version-stamped.
- RHEL administration content — command intent, notes, "changed in RHEL X" notes,
  and flag explanations — is paraphrased from Red Hat Enterprise Linux product
  documentation and from the man pages and --help output of the installed
  binaries on the host that was read. Every entry carries a `source` field naming
  the origin, a retrieval date, and a license_class. Raw vendor text is kept in
  content-src/ for review and licensing audit and is NEVER embedded in the
  shipped artifact: only original paraphrase plus citation reaches the file.
- Error signatures, decision trees, and destructive-command patterns are original
  work informed by the referenced documentation and operational experience.

